"selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7829ef1a6190c798f17ac2c842e68655c11840f858b1476ddb0805138140400" "basis" 1528 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(3)=2,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8bdfaa3991318a59dd6533749b71d62590fa2142e8e2ebcf14cc48a77981c44"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={1}^{2}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={2}^{2}-{1}^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={5}^{2}-{2}^{2}=21 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=21\\cdot 1=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+21=26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $21$ | $21$ |\n| $3$ | $26$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈26.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8bdfaa3991318a59dd6533749b71d62590fa2142e8e2ebcf14cc48a77981c44"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25"] ["math" "26"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "29"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-117952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "26"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8bdfaa3991318a59dd6533749b71d62590fa2142e8e2ebcf14cc48a77981c44"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=1\\cdot \\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $2+2\\sqrt{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2+2\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8bdfaa3991318a59dd6533749b71d62590fa2142e8e2ebcf14cc48a77981c44"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2+2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "q-120939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=0+(-1)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y={1}^{2}+(-2)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065687/state.json" "file_sha256" "f62b48b464396e313a1894c0f71aed059c463fc22a07747f49094793ce94d548"} {"question" "